1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now, the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Here comes a big pun. Actually, two of them, two campaign hats go out for a hike.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Two campaign hats go out for a hike.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,000
One of them is worn out and the other goes on ahead.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Hey, if you pretty sure you heard that one before, you did.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
You heard it a few months ago because this is a rebroadcast.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
And this is podcast number 80.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Hey, this is Clark Green. Welcome back to the Scout Master podcast.

9
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Yep, it is summertime.

10
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
We're off traveling and scouting and doing all kinds of great stuff.

11
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
So we're rebroadcasting some of our favorite podcasts from over the past year.

12
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
And in this particular podcast, we're going to be speaking with Rob Ferris and Harry Wimbrough,

13
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
who are two of the three authors of Working the Patrol Method,

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
a book that we've talked about for quite a bit on the podcast and on the blog.

15
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
You'll be able to find links on the blog where you can purchase the book

16
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
and some of the other information that's mentioned in the course of the interview.

17
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
This first broadcast back in October of 2010.

18
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
So I think it's worth listening to a couple more times and I hope you agree with me.

19
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:03,000
And I'll be back after the interview.

20
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
So here we go. Let's get started, shall we?

21
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Hey, this is Clark Green with the Scout Master podcast,

22
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
and I am happy to have on Skype with me Rob Ferris and Harry Wimbrough,

23
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
two of the three authors of a book called Working the Patrol Method.

24
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:35,000
How are you guys doing this evening?

25
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,000
We're great.

26
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Rob, you've got five kits from what I can tell.

27
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:43,000
That's right. I do. I do.

28
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
One of them is an Eagle Scout and the others are on the way, huh?

29
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Well, except for my daughter.

30
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Well, yeah.

31
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
She was in part inspiration for what we wrote.

32
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
She's been a constant supporter and she actually reviewed a part of the books.

33
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
You became an Eagle Scout back in the late 60s, early 70s,

34
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
and then when your oldest son turned 11, you got back in.

35
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Just briefly tell me what this statement in the book means.

36
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:14,000
It says Wood Badge changed your life.

37
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Wood Badge was what made me finally get the Patrol Method inside and out,

38
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,000
and it was just an incredible experience for me.

39
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
It allowed us to actually practice the Patrol Method as adults

40
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
and then to step back and actually realize,

41
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
okay, how do we create this kind of teamwork?

42
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,000
How do we create this camaraderie with our Scouts back in our units?

43
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:44,000
It was an amazing experience.

44
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Let me also introduce Harry, Harry Wimbrough,

45
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
and you also became an Eagle Scout in the mid-70s?

46
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Yes, 1975.

47
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
When you wrote the introduction to this book, your son Joe was on his way.

48
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Has he made it yet?

49
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:08,000
I keep reminding him on a daily basis that his time is running out.

50
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:15,000
He's a senior in high school, plays in three different bands, plays the flute,

51
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:22,000
and has a girlfriend, so I remind him that his time is running out.

52
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
You and two million other dads are doing the exact same thing.

53
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Well, even though I made my Eagle in 75,

54
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:40,000
what a lot of people don't know is I set my board or got presented my Eagle

55
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
after I had already completed basic AIT for the Army.

56
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Oh, no kidding. So you were right up against it too, huh?

57
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Yes, I was right up against it.

58
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Well, your secret is safe with me. I will not tell Joe that.

59
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
I'll bet he already knows, though.

60
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,000
He already knows.

61
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Oh, I want to also mention Ted Knight, who is one of your co-authors.

62
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:13,000
You had the three of you author this, and unfortunately, Ted couldn't join us this evening.

63
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:19,000
The book is called Working the Patrol Method, and there was a specific reason that you stayed in the book

64
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
for having gotten it started and taking it all the way through to press.

65
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Well, tell me guys, who is Bryson Bort?

66
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Bryson Bort is an Eagle alumni of our unit. He joined the United States Army.

67
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
He was stationed with the Army where he was exercising a leadership position.

68
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
As he got into that and started trying to do his job effectively,

69
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
what he realized was that many, many things that he learned in Boy Scouts

70
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:57,000
were invaluable to him in assisting him and informing him as to how to be a leader in the United States Army.

71
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:04,000
You begin in the first pages of the book with an email that you got from Bryson.

72
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
It is a very inspirational introduction to the book. It really kind of sets the tone.

73
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Why don't you describe exactly what you were after in going through the work to write this book?

74
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:17,000
It must have taken ages.

75
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,000
It was a labor of love, Clark, but it actually literally took some years.

76
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:29,000
It took us about four years to put it all together.

77
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:37,000
The stories that are in the book are actual stories that we could take and mold and write about

78
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,000
how the adult leader coached and mentored and taught the young scout.

79
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
What do we call those in the book here? Campfire yarns?

80
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Yarns, just as Maiden Powell called his stories yarns. We adopt his terminology.

81
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:59,000
They are tremendously helpful in kind of putting some meat on the bones of the concepts that you're talking about

82
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
because a lot of what was in them was very familiar to me as a scout master.

83
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Pretty typical stories that happen in scout troops all over the country.

84
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Actually, a lot of what scout masters will see or I'm experienced scout masters will see in our book.

85
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:21,000
As you say, Clark will be very familiar. It will be situations that they've confronted themselves

86
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
or that they've seen other leaders in their units confront.

87
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:31,000
One of our goals is to suggest at least what's the most appropriate or effective

88
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:36,000
or at least some ideas for some good ways to respond or to react

89
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,000
that will further the goal of leadership development.

90
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Scouting is a game of the purpose.

91
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:53,000
In scout leadership, for youth leadership, you have to turn it into a game for them

92
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:58,000
so that they don't really realize that they're actually learning something, but it's a game.

93
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:05,000
The yarns are a good way to start. Everybody likes a good story.

94
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
There's a lot that I've read in the book that I've written on the blog in years past.

95
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
We're all taking the lead from good old Baden Powell.

96
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:20,000
It's not unusual that we should have some of the same ideas and things, but it's been pretty amazing.

97
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:28,000
It's not, of course, that any of us are thinking that any of this is particularly unique.

98
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
As you say, this is what Baden Powell intended.

99
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:33,000
Yeah, he was there before any of us.

100
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:39,000
He defined all this. It seems like that at least some of us in the Scouting organization

101
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:47,000
have gotten away from some of the basic things that were probably very clearly evident in 1911.

102
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:54,000
It's just not as emphasized in the training materials, and it's kind of there, but it's not highlighted.

103
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,000
You know, I teach, this is a area, I teach scout master specifics.

104
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
And the piece for the patrol method is, you know, you get 20 minutes.

105
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
You just hit the highlights. You really can't teach the scout masters everything that they really need to know.

106
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:16,000
You talk about leadership training and mentoring as being something that is hands-on and not theoretical.

107
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:21,000
You take a young scout. He's been in the classroom all day long.

108
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:26,000
So when he comes to a scout meeting, you don't want to sit down and talk leadership.

109
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:36,000
You want to put some of it into practical application, but you allow that 11, 12, 13-year-old boy that's in charge of his patrol,

110
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
you know, kind of make a mistake and then you're pulling off to the side and you say,

111
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
okay, what could you have done a little bit better?

112
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,000
What went right? What went wrong?

113
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
And what can you do to improve on it the next time?

114
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:57,000
You let the boy kind of make those decisions, but you as the leader, don't make them boring.

115
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Harry, you're telling me that these guys are going to make mistakes?

116
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:05,000
You know, I don't know, but too many leaders that don't make mistakes every day.

117
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:11,000
We as the leaders have to be there to kind of pick them back up and say, okay, you made a mistake.

118
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:17,000
All right, so yeah, you got to be there to kind of coach them along and show them maybe a different direction.

119
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:25,000
You spend a lot of time in the book talking history. Why is it important that we understand the history of scouting?

120
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:32,000
Baden Powell lived 100 years ago, and so you could look back and say, okay, well, yeah, scouting is traditional, of course.

121
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:34,000
We've got all these great traditions.

122
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
Baden Powell wrote almost a century ago.

123
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
What does that really have to do with what's going on in the 21st century?

124
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:50,000
And the fact of the matter is that if you go back and read what Baden Powell went through in order to create scouting,

125
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:55,000
what influences did he have upon himself? Where did this all come from?

126
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:04,000
What you end up finding is that he had a mixture of remarkable influences and out of it, out of all of that came what he referred to.

127
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:12,000
As an experimental summer camp at Brown Sea Island, it wasn't just the first Boy Scout summer camp.

128
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:18,000
It was actually the model for the successful implementation of the patrol method.

129
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:25,000
That has never been done before. I mean, he was the first to do that, and it was revolutionary.

130
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:30,000
If you want to be an effective leader, you've got to know a little bit about scouting.

131
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:39,000
You've got to learn about Baden Powell and some of the other great leaders that we've had within the scouting movement.

132
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:44,000
We teach U.S. history in our schools. Why is that?

133
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:50,000
So that we can understand why our forefathers wrote the Constitution and the Declaration of Independence.

134
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:55,000
There's something about the history of scouting that helps put things in context for us.

135
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:02,000
And then I was very impressed with the statement that you make in the book about stewardship and the stewardship of the scouting movement.

136
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,000
What can you tell me about that?

137
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:17,000
Modern scout leaders are actually following in the footsteps of a century of highly illustrious, highly motivated, highly passionate and experienced scout leaders that went before us.

138
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,000
We are the ones who have now been handed the torch. We scout leaders.

139
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:32,000
And we owe those in the program who came before us the responsibility to carry that torch as effectively as possible into this new century.

140
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:42,000
Okay, so let me give you this scenario. I'm a brand new newly minted scout leader and you've got 30 seconds to talk to me.

141
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:44,000
What are you going to tell me to do?

142
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,000
Read the book.

143
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Go talk to your SPL.

144
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
And ask the SPL what he wants you to do.

145
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:58,000
And hopefully the SPL will tell you to go sit in the corner. If we need your help, we'll let you know.

146
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:06,000
What we say on page 10 of the book is a paragraph in there which actually arguably tells it all.

147
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:13,000
It says, leadership training and mentoring in a scout troop is actually very simple.

148
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:19,000
Any scout master who puts his mind to it can do it. Trust and respect your scouts.

149
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Use the patrol method. Give your scouts real responsibility, mentor, motivate, recognize results.

150
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:34,000
What we would tell a new scout leader is do those things. Those are the important things to do.

151
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Look at the program from the standpoint of the boys and do those things.

152
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:51,000
But in order to do those things right requires either experience, which obviously takes some time to get,

153
00:13:52,000 --> 00:14:00,000
or you can also go and talk to your scout master or district training people or other resources.

154
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:05,000
And we also quote another thing in this book that Will Rogers once said.

155
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:09,000
He said, there are three kinds of men. The ones that learn by reading, the few who learn by observation,

156
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,000
and the rest of us have to pee on the electric fence and find out for ourselves.

157
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:23,000
And that's what happens to most scout masters. It's a trial and error occupation, I guess you could say.

158
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:30,000
So what we'd like to do is to give some new leaders a shortcut rather than having to learn everything the hard way.

159
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:35,000
Maybe we can give them a sort of a leg up on the learning curve as well as an orientation.

160
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:42,000
So instead of heading off in a direction other than the one that will lead them to a scout led and scout run troop,

161
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:50,000
we can sort of orient them in the right direction that in their journey in that direction toward a scout led troop

162
00:14:50,000 --> 00:15:00,000
that they will find a more rewarding and more effective leadership role for themselves.

163
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:05,000
In particular, they'll be much more effective in teaching their scouts how to become leaders.

164
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:12,000
So at one point you guys were both newly minted adult leaders and you jumped in with both feet.

165
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:20,000
What has changed about your approach from that first day to what you're doing now?

166
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:27,000
I think we can both say that radical changes have occurred since each of us began being scout leaders.

167
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:37,000
We are both very energetic, sort of potentially do it all kind of people who have ideas.

168
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:50,000
And I think that when we both started as newly minted adult leaders, we were not effective in terms of developing a scout led and scout run program.

169
00:15:51,000 --> 00:16:01,000
We were acting as patrol leaders ourselves as adult patrol leaders as opposed to scout leaders who will allow the boys to be the patrol leaders and the boys to be the leaders.

170
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Well, isn't it kind of messy when you let the boys do that?

171
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:10,000
That's the fun part. That's the fun part.

172
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:17,000
But I'm a retired command sergeant major after 30 years in the Army, a leader.

173
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:24,000
They say the big difference between the Army and Boy Scouts is that the Boy Scouts are adult leaders.

174
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:31,000
Every time one of my non-commissioned officers made a mistake, if I crucified them, they wouldn't learn.

175
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:36,000
They would shut down. And I find the same thing with the boys.

176
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:45,000
The boys, if you always are telling them that this is what you did wrong, what you did wrong, what you did wrong, then they're going to stop doing anything.

177
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:54,000
Because all they know is I'm just going to get a hollered at by Mr. Wimbrell because it's not being done his way.

178
00:16:54,000 --> 00:17:06,000
And that's where I had to step back and really take a look and kind of adjust my leadership style from the Army to the Boy Scouts to scouting.

179
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
And it works in both places?

180
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:16,000
Yes. It works in scouting and it worked in the Army too.

181
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:23,000
When I first became a non-commissioned officer at the age of 19, I was a pretty hard charging NCO.

182
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:33,000
And I had a wise squad leader of the tune, so I pulled me off to the side and said, you know, Sergeant Wimbrell says, you're going to go far in this man's Army.

183
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:39,000
However, you need to change some of your leadership style.

184
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:41,000
So I did.

185
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:46,000
There was a particularly interesting section of the book.

186
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:52,000
And section seven is called Planning, How to Facilitate Without Taking Over.

187
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:57,000
How do you facilitate youth planning without just kind of jumping in and doing it for them?

188
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:03,000
Our experience, Clark, is that that actually is one of the most difficult things for a scout master to do.

189
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:12,000
Scouts that are aged, you know, 14, 15, a troll leader age are not generally very good at long-range planning.

190
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
Their time window for planning is totally different from adults.

191
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:22,000
You know, oftentimes, scouts are worrying about what they're going to have for lunch, you know, five minutes before they're supposed to eat it.

192
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:30,000
We're asking them to do some planning where, so it's October, next June, we're going to go to, you know, we're going to go caving next June.

193
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:34,000
And we better start making the arrangements and they'll look at you like you have to take the head exam.

194
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:36,000
That's next June.

195
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:38,000
What are you talking about?

196
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:40,000
Why are we worried about that?

197
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:42,000
That's a lifetime away.

198
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:48,000
If you're a program director for a camperee, you're planning that camperee a year out.

199
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:56,000
And you know about it at troop meetings and the boys, just an example, we had a camperee last weekend.

200
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
The boys started really planning for it two weeks ago.

201
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:10,000
And then the meeting before the camperees, they finalized their meal plans and figured out who was going to buy what food and what time they were going to meet.

202
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
They had it well under control.

203
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
As adults, we do long-term planning and short-term planning.

204
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:23,000
The boys plan, when am I going to eat? When am I going to sleep? When am I going to eat? When am I going to sleep?

205
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:28,000
I have a pair of socks. It's good. I don't have to worry about anything else.

206
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
Have you been talking to my scouts, Harry?

207
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:33,000
And I've been talking to my son.

208
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:42,000
But one thing is the boys, we're going to go camping, but they don't think about the tour permits.

209
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:47,000
Safety forms and all the health forms and stuff like that.

210
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:56,000
And that's where you've got to kind of coach them along on that area until when they start getting used to doing some of the planning,

211
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:03,000
then they can say, oh, we need to talk to somebody on the committee to get our tour permit.

212
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
We need to make sure that we have the permission forms.

213
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:13,000
But it takes time and it takes the coaching and the mentoring of an adult leader to be able to do that.

214
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:22,000
And it's actually a very valuable skill that an adult leader will be teaching his scouts in taking them through that exercise.

215
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,000
It seems to us that there's some pretty important things involved.

216
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:29,000
One of them is the expectation of how much planning.

217
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:41,000
So adults have an expectation of probably much more intense and much more detailed planning than any scout is going to have unless maybe they're an older scout.

218
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:52,000
And also one of the points we make in our book is that, well, depending upon what it is, you may not need that intense level of planning.

219
00:20:53,000 --> 00:21:01,000
So for example, if you're a type A scout master and you don't want to disappoint the parents by making your unit look like it's disorganized,

220
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:06,000
I mean, everything organized and running like clockwork, that means you need a very detailed plan.

221
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:09,000
So that way everyone knows everything that's going to happen in advance.

222
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:13,000
Then our suggestion is, well, okay, look at what the objective is.

223
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:19,000
So if it's just a weekend camp out at a local park or whatever, do you need that level of planning?

224
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:28,000
Or would you rather leave it up a little bit up in the air and let the scouts work through it and then actually learn from the experience?

225
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:30,000
Sometimes you do need that level of planning.

226
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:38,000
If you're going to be doing something pretty intense, you know, some kind of high adventure program where we are worried about health and safety kind of issues, absolutely.

227
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:43,000
An adult needs to make sure that those details are locked down and solid.

228
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:59,000
But it does seem to be helpful for a scout master to talk with the parents and to create an expectation that, look, this is not an adult run.

229
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
This is not an adult planned unit here.

230
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:10,000
We give our scouts major responsibility for planning and execution.

231
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:16,000
And if things don't go 100% perfectly, well, guess what?

232
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
That's the way it's supposed to be.

233
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:23,000
And as long as the boys have a good time, they probably planned it that way.

234
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
Well, I'm going to be the devil's advocate here for a moment.

235
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:35,000
I mean, honestly, guys, I can expect a 15, 16, 17 year old scout to be thinking about things like tour permits and permission slips and stuff like that.

236
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:37,000
I mean, I'm the scout master.

237
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:39,000
Isn't that my job?

238
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:41,000
Isn't that what I'm supposed to be doing?

239
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
So at that point, it ends up being an issue of responsibility.

240
00:22:44,000 --> 00:23:05,000
And we agree, Clark, that it's going to be probably an undue expectation for a 14, 15 year old scout to be worried about a tour permit and whether all the medical forms are filed and are brought along with the unit.

241
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:09,000
But at the same time, they shouldn't be oblivious to it either.

242
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:17,000
Maybe he's not getting the tour permit, but he knows the adult on the committee that gets the tour permits for us.

243
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:27,000
And that becomes a teaching point that teaching and coaching, you know, you might want to say, hey, have you talked to the tour permit committee member?

244
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:32,000
So it becomes more of a teaching point than really for him to be a plan.

245
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:35,000
But it's something for him to think about as he goes on.

246
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:45,000
So maybe the next time when he does as a patrol leader plans and adding, you know, I need to talk to Mr. Smith about tour permits.

247
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:49,000
I can see that, Harry, but like I said, I'm still being the devil's advocate here.

248
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:51,000
That makes sense.

249
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:53,000
But isn't that just as a scout master?

250
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:55,000
Isn't that just easier for me to do myself?

251
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:57,000
Yeah, it'd be easier for you to do it yourself.

252
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:01,000
But what are you teaching the boy to do it yourself?

253
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:07,000
That's actually a great comment, because you could extend that to just about everything we're talking about.

254
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
Isn't it easier for the scout master to do it himself?

255
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:13,000
Isn't it easier for the scout master to define the whole plan for the whole year?

256
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:18,000
Isn't it easier for the scout master to tell patrol leaders exactly what they're supposed to be doing?

257
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:24,000
Isn't it easier to just bypass the patrol leaders and tell the scouts precisely what they're supposed to be doing?

258
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:26,000
That's not the point at all.

259
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:28,000
It is easier.

260
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:32,000
It is easier for a scout master to have an adult run troop.

261
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:35,000
There is no doubt in my mind that that's the case.

262
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
It is easier for a scout master to have an adult run troop.

263
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:45,000
Much less trouble, much less coordination, much more straightforward to have, I'm sorry, to have an adult run troop.

264
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
A scout run troop, that's hard.

265
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:57,000
A scout run troop is harder because now the adult, now the scout leaders have to start thinking about their interventions.

266
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:06,000
Do we really want to step in and command this or direct that or structure this?

267
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:12,000
Or is this something that the scouts are able to do, perfectly capable of doing?

268
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:17,000
And yet if we step in as adults, the scouts will just back right off and let the adult do it.

269
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:22,000
I think most experienced scout masters would almost certainly say the same thing.

270
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:25,000
Oh yeah, adult run troops, that's easy.

271
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:27,000
Much easier.

272
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:31,000
Certainly a lot less wear and tear on the scout masters' nerves.

273
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:33,000
Right, and it also takes less time.

274
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:37,000
The fact of the matter is that it takes more time to have a scout run troop.

275
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:41,000
And actually Baden Powell specifically said that.

276
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:46,000
One of the things that he wrote, I can't quote it exactly, but he said something up to paraphrase.

277
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:52,000
He said that what you're supposed to do is to allow your patrol leaders to do as much as possible.

278
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:56,000
It's not so much to save the scout master time.

279
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
It's to train the scout.

280
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:00,000
And that's what the point is.

281
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:12,000
I continue in my advocacy role for the devil, but parents in our troop are just not going to put up with me sitting around and not doing these things.

282
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:18,000
My committee is going to go crazy if I tell them that we're going to let the boys plan and lead these things.

283
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:20,000
They're expecting me to do that.

284
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:22,000
I mean, how do I handle a situation like that?

285
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:27,000
As the scout master, you have to tell them, you know, go sit in the corner.

286
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:29,000
Let the boys do it.

287
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:33,000
Let the boys come to you and ask you for what they need from the committee.

288
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:41,000
The committee is there to support parents do more for their children today than when you and I were growing up.

289
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:55,000
And so they have to allow their boys to spread their wings a little bit earlier than what they normally would at the age of 18 or 18 or 19.

290
00:26:56,000 --> 00:27:00,000
And so you just got to pull them and say, this is a boy.

291
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:02,000
Let the boys do it.

292
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:07,000
Yes, it may look like chaos, but it's a boy controlled chaos.

293
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:18,000
The other thing that I would say is that that I agree, Clark, that is that your question is one of the biggest quandaries of a scout master who wants to have a scout led troop.

294
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:20,000
Wait a minute. I'm the scout master.

295
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:23,000
All the parents are expecting me to be the leader.

296
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
All the scouts are expecting me to be the leader.

297
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:29,000
The program is expecting me to be the leader.

298
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:31,000
That means what?

299
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:37,000
Does that mean that that I as scout master need to stand up in front, make all the all the important decisions?

300
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:41,000
Or is there another approach where it's the boys who are up in front?

301
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:45,000
It's the boys who are making the important decisions.

302
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
It's the boys who are the most visible leaders.

303
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:53,000
And I'll just quote from something which Ronald Reagan said, we've got this quote in our book as well.

304
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:58,000
He says, there's no limit to what a man can do or where he can go.

305
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:01,000
If he doesn't mind, who gets the credit?

306
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:09,000
The scouts are the ones who should be getting the credit for the successes and taking the responsibilities for things that don't work out all that well.

307
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:21,000
So if I invest my scouts with the authority and the responsibility to plan these things, aren't they going to miss some stuff?

308
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:27,000
I mean, wouldn't they get more out of it if I was planning outings and things?

309
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:29,000
Aren't they going to miss opportunities?

310
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:31,000
Just the opposite.

311
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:33,000
Just the opposite.

312
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:48,000
If I, if I as the adult leader plan everything, you know, being the type of person that I am, I've written a five paragraph operations order and, you know, have all my execution checklists and execution matrix.

313
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:53,000
So I know everything is going, I got to have this, this and everything is going well.

314
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:55,000
Yeah. Okay. Great.

315
00:28:56,000 --> 00:29:03,000
But if the boys plan it and say they, you know, they're going to learn, learn from their mistakes.

316
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:12,000
They actually might miss some opportunities in one respect and gain immeasurably other opportunities on the other side.

317
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:32,000
So in a, in a world where our scouts, at least many of them in many parts of the countries are expecting to be entertained by adults and programmed up the wazoo, out the wazoo by the adults to put the scouts in charge and have responsibility for, for figuring out what they're going to do.

318
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:34,000
That's an opportunity.

319
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:42,000
If I'm not making sure that it's, you know, really a whiz bang program and everything, aren't they going to just leave the troop?

320
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:50,000
How am I going to attract new scouts if the scouts are just caught up in leading and doing all this stuff all the time?

321
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:52,000
I mean, they won't be doing anything fun.

322
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:59,000
They are doing fun things because they're the ones that are planning the program with the resources that you've given them.

323
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:13,000
With the boys planning the program, with the boys executing their program, that's what's going to keep them active because if you as the adult planet, they're saying, huh, okay, yep, here we go again.

324
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:16,000
We're doing the same, same stuff and we're not doing what we want to do.

325
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:23,000
And the other thing I would add is that it's not like the scout master just sits back and lets the program fall apart.

326
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:30,000
You might need to do that on occasion in order to make sure the boys understand that it's their responsibility that if they don't do it, it's not happening.

327
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:38,000
But this is where the mentoring and the coaching and the advising comes in where you don't tell them what to do.

328
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:44,000
You, you do what any respectful leader will do when training a new leader.

329
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:48,000
Okay. Let me show you some resources like your scout development.

330
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:56,000
And at that point, you're not the one who's dictating it. He's deciding it. He's got responsibility for it. He'll execute it. It'll be great.

331
00:30:57,000 --> 00:30:59,000
He'll take the credit for it. He'll have a big smile on his face.

332
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:04,000
He'll have learned a ton of stuff about himself and about how to be an effective leader.

333
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:16,000
So you guys must have a troop of just who are some kind of superhero scouts because my scouts, they wouldn't be able to do this, would they?

334
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:25,000
You know, any scouts would be able to do it. And no, our own troop is not superheroes or anything like that.

335
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:38,000
So obviously it doesn't always work 100% because basically these scouts who are planning these program events are inexperienced and they have a lot to learn.

336
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:53,000
And so in the course of learning, they may not be able to put on something that is as perfect and clockwork as an experienced type A adult who is planning the whole thing from beginning to end and thought of every detail.

337
00:31:54,000 --> 00:32:05,000
It's just not going to be like that. It's more going to be like, you know, making sausage as opposed to having some, you know, pristine, elegant, you know, finished product that just handed off to the scout and say, okay, you guys execute this.

338
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:32,000
If you do this, it'll be great. But you are sacrificing something. There's no doubt about it. In a scout, run troop, at least until you end up being able to retain the 16 and 17-year-olds who are now in the habit of taking responsibility and making these kind of decisions.

339
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:51,000
There's going to be a lag. And this is where persistence comes in. This is where you have to keep your eye on the objective as a scoutmaster. What are you trying to accomplish here? Are you trying to have a 100% perfect program or are you trying to, you know, train new leaders?

340
00:32:51,000 --> 00:33:03,000
So I've been to the training and I watched the videos and everything looks really perfect in those videos and I showed this to my scouts and I went and I worked with them on this and they're looking at it like a dog with a new pan.

341
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:11,000
They don't seem to understand any of it. They don't plan anything. They don't do anything. So what am I supposed to do?

342
00:33:12,000 --> 00:33:13,000
Yeah, I mean, that's a tough one.

343
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:34,000
Yeah, but maybe what you have to do is take a boy that's not a patrol leader yet but is maybe a first-class scout. He wants to be a patrol leader. So you're sending off to the NYLT, the National Youth Leadership Force.

344
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:52,000
That will kind of get him excited about the program. He brings it back and kind of making like a leadership trainer so that he can an advisor to other patrol leaders, kind of like a troop guide for leadership.

345
00:33:53,000 --> 00:34:02,000
If the boys just want to sit around and let the adults do it, then what you do is you plan a weekend where you just go and you sit around.

346
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:15,000
And when the boys say, well, this wasn't very much fun, then you say, okay, if you had a plan something, we might have been able to have some fun activities while we were on this camping trip.

347
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:27,000
But you left it all up to me as the adult. The only thing I wanted to do was go out to the woods and sit on the rock next to the fire. For me, that's a fun time.

348
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:41,000
But maybe before that, you have a conversation with your patrol leaders council. Say, gentlemen, this is your weekend. You're going to plan this weekend.

349
00:34:42,000 --> 00:34:52,000
And whatever you plan within reason, as long as there's no issues with health and safety, I just got a master going to go along with it.

350
00:34:53,000 --> 00:35:05,000
But it's your responsibility to plan. And so you challenge them and make them understand that maybe for the first time in their whole lives, they're able to actually plan their own time for a whole weekend.

351
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:18,000
They get to do what they want to do. It's their responsibility and it's their privilege to do it. And no adult is going to tell them, oh, you can't do that. You can't do this. You have to do this and you have to do that.

352
00:35:19,000 --> 00:35:31,000
And that's an amazing opportunity for a 21st century, you know, 15, because most of them, many of them, at least in many parts of the country, many of them never get that opportunity.

353
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:44,000
If I do this, if I tell them that they can do anything they want to do, all they're going to do is sit around and play video games and, you know, they'll just eat whatever they want to eat and they probably won't get any requirements done.

354
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:51,000
And, you know, so how in the world can I just let them have carte blanche to go and do whatever they want to do?

355
00:35:51,000 --> 00:36:02,000
If you just go and do requirements for the weekend, well, that's not the right way. You go and you do an event and then you have them look at their book and see what requirements that they might have did.

356
00:36:03,000 --> 00:36:08,000
And the other thing is that if you challenge your scouts to come up with something, you'll be amazed.

357
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:18,000
Exactly, you'll be amazed. Sometimes, often, actually, they will come up with something that you as a scout measure would never have thought of in a million years.

358
00:36:18,000 --> 00:36:27,000
And it's not because it's not appropriate. It's not because it's not safe. It's entirely safe and it's entirely appropriate and it's theirs.

359
00:36:28,000 --> 00:36:43,000
It's not your idea. It's their idea. And it might not be something that you as a, you know, as a 40 or 50 or whatever your old scout master might think is what those boys are going to want to do.

360
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:52,000
So if you had been dictating it, you would never in a million years have thought of doing that. But it ends up being a blast for them. They absolutely love it and they own it.

361
00:36:53,000 --> 00:36:54,000
It's theirs and they own it.

362
00:36:55,000 --> 00:37:10,000
We're not wired the same way as a 14, 15-year-old is. They're wired just a little bit different. And sometimes it's hard to remember that we were that 13, 14-year-old boys doing the same thing.

363
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:17,000
Talk to me a little bit about, you have a section about caring leadership. What is caring leadership?

364
00:37:18,000 --> 00:37:36,000
Tell leaders three things. One, lead from the front, set the example and take care of your people. You've got to know them. Everybody that's in your patrol is a valuable member. But you as a leader, you have to know what's, you know, what's his likes and dislikes.

365
00:37:37,000 --> 00:37:49,000
And caring leadership actually is one of the most important things to get across to our young leaders because it really is part of the key to the whole thing.

366
00:37:49,000 --> 00:38:00,000
Anyone who's studied and worked with adolescents, anyone who's tried to have anything to do with the scout troop knows that a 14 or 15-year-old boy is pretty self-absorbed.

367
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:05,000
They're thinking, oftentimes, they're thinking about one person and that's themselves.

368
00:38:06,000 --> 00:38:17,000
To get them to think about the 11-year-old who's just joined the troop and needs some mentoring and some help, that's important.

369
00:38:18,000 --> 00:38:30,000
Once again, that's something that at least some scouts, it's going to be a real revelation for them that they're supposed to be thinking about younger scouts.

370
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:35,000
They're supposed to be caring about those younger kids, but that's what our program is based on.

371
00:38:36,000 --> 00:38:45,000
I think, fellas, that we've just scratched the surface of what's in the book and it's arranged in a way that's very accessible.

372
00:38:46,000 --> 00:38:49,000
I think it's just an excellent resource for scout leaders.

373
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:51,000
So let me ask you this, guys.

374
00:38:52,000 --> 00:38:58,000
If you get discouraged and you get down and things aren't going quite so well as a scout leader,

375
00:38:58,000 --> 00:39:02,000
what makes you keep at it?

376
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:06,000
What do you think about that kind of reanimate you and get you moving again?

377
00:39:07,000 --> 00:39:13,000
Although we can take inspiration from people like Baden Powell, and that certainly is one place to go,

378
00:39:14,000 --> 00:39:24,000
but the other place to go is just to watch your boys watch how they're interacting with one another and reflect on the huge potential there.

379
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:29,000
And that you as a scout leader can have a major impact on their lives.

380
00:39:30,000 --> 00:39:35,000
And so when you start thinking about that, it's pretty hard to stay discouraged for long.

381
00:39:36,000 --> 00:39:47,000
When I get discouraged, one of the things that keeps me going is when you sit in a scout master conference and you talk to a boy

382
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:54,000
and you're asking what he wants to do and he says, I want to be an Eagle Scout.

383
00:39:55,000 --> 00:39:58,000
And you're asking, well, why do you want to make an Eagle Scout?

384
00:39:59,000 --> 00:40:02,000
He says, because scouting and Eagle Scouts make a difference.

385
00:40:03,000 --> 00:40:07,000
And that kind of rejuvenates me a little bit.

386
00:40:07,000 --> 00:40:15,000
And just looking at the boys and their smiles at the end of a camping trip,

387
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:20,000
when it's been raining and you've been miserable and they say, oh man, was that a great time or what?

388
00:40:21,000 --> 00:40:26,000
The best times always have rain involved, apparently.

389
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:28,000
I mean, that's my experience.

390
00:40:29,000 --> 00:40:30,000
Or snow.

391
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:32,000
Yeah.

392
00:40:33,000 --> 00:40:34,000
Rob, I'll start with you.

393
00:40:35,000 --> 00:40:36,000
Tell me your best scouting moment.

394
00:40:37,000 --> 00:40:56,000
I would have to say is a series of discrete events where Scouts have come up to me after they have been able to lead activities

395
00:40:57,000 --> 00:41:01,000
and come up to me and say, Mr. Ferris, that was the best.

396
00:41:02,000 --> 00:41:04,000
That was the best thing we've ever done in scouting.

397
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:10,000
Those moments are just exhilarating.

398
00:41:11,000 --> 00:41:16,000
You just feel on the top of the world that you've made a difference.

399
00:41:17,000 --> 00:41:18,000
So, Harry, you're up.

400
00:41:19,000 --> 00:41:20,000
What's your best scouting moment?

401
00:41:21,000 --> 00:41:29,000
It actually happened last year when I was able to, I was a crew advisor for a crew that went to Fillmont.

402
00:41:29,000 --> 00:41:39,000
And finally, towards the end, the crew came together as one.

403
00:41:40,000 --> 00:41:49,000
And they really got it on the bus ride when they presented me with a belt buckle.

404
00:41:50,000 --> 00:41:54,000
I finally realized that they finally got it and what it really meant.

405
00:41:55,000 --> 00:41:57,000
That was probably one of my greatest scouting moments.

406
00:41:58,000 --> 00:42:02,000
The book is available at scoutleadership.com.

407
00:42:03,000 --> 00:42:08,000
You can order with a PayPal account or a credit card and you'll be able to get the book.

408
00:42:09,000 --> 00:42:11,000
And it's a very modest 1998 with shipping.

409
00:42:12,000 --> 00:42:16,000
You're taking some of the profits from this and dedicating them to the troop.

410
00:42:17,000 --> 00:42:18,000
Is that right?

411
00:42:19,000 --> 00:42:22,000
Actually, not just the troop, but some other worthy scouting organization.

412
00:42:23,000 --> 00:42:24,000
So, that's absolutely right.

413
00:42:24,000 --> 00:42:28,000
We wrote this book not to make a profit, I guarantee you.

414
00:42:29,000 --> 00:42:35,000
Our main interest of this book is to get the word out on the patrol method,

415
00:42:36,000 --> 00:42:46,000
to try and leave our own legacy by getting that word out to 21st century scout leaders.

416
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:48,000
So, we're trying to make a difference.

417
00:42:49,000 --> 00:42:51,000
And that's the only reason why we undertook this endeavor.

418
00:42:51,000 --> 00:42:56,000
And if you want five or more copies, there's a discount available. Is that right?

419
00:42:57,000 --> 00:42:58,000
Absolutely.

420
00:42:59,000 --> 00:43:04,000
You can just send us an email and we're happy to accommodate.

421
00:43:05,000 --> 00:43:06,000
Yeah, to work through that.

422
00:43:07,000 --> 00:43:10,000
Well, I'll tell you, it comes with my highest recommendations.

423
00:43:11,000 --> 00:43:16,000
I'll have a link to it on the blog, folks, so that you can find your way to a place where you can order the book.

424
00:43:17,000 --> 00:43:20,000
And I really appreciate having talked with you, Rob and Harry.

425
00:43:21,000 --> 00:43:22,000
It's always good to talk to scouts.

426
00:43:23,000 --> 00:43:26,000
Once again, congratulations on a piece of work well done.

427
00:43:27,000 --> 00:43:29,000
Yeah, it's our privilege to talk with you.

428
00:43:30,000 --> 00:43:33,000
Thank you so much for discovering us and you know, tracking us down here for doing this.

429
00:43:34,000 --> 00:43:35,000
This is, uh...

430
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:57,000
Well, thanks for listening to this edition of the Scoutmaster podcast.

431
00:43:58,000 --> 00:44:00,000
A rebroadcast, but here's how to keep in touch.

432
00:44:01,000 --> 00:44:04,000
You can read the Scoutmaster blog at scoutmastercg.com.

433
00:44:05,000 --> 00:44:08,000
And when you go there, you can also subscribe to the Scoutmaster newsletter,

434
00:44:09,000 --> 00:44:15,000
which is a monthly digest of the Scoutmaster blog and podcast with some great scouting resources around a monthly theme.

435
00:44:16,000 --> 00:44:19,000
This letter includes a giveaway from one of our affiliate sponsors.

436
00:44:27,000 --> 00:44:30,000
You can subscribe to the Scoutmaster podcast on iTunes,

437
00:44:31,000 --> 00:44:33,000
and when you do, feel free to leave a comment or review or a rating.

438
00:44:34,000 --> 00:44:42,000
And you can call to leave a voicemail comment or question at 484-734-0002.

439
00:44:47,000 --> 00:44:56,000
You can also follow us at Scoutmaster blog on Facebook and Scoutmastercg on Twitter.

440
00:44:57,000 --> 00:45:05,000
Finally, you can email me, Clark Green, with your comments or questions at scoutmastercg, at Verizon.net.

441
00:45:06,000 --> 00:45:09,000
That's scoutmastercg, at Verizon.net.

442
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:21,000
The Scoutmaster blog and the Scoutmaster podcast are not official publications of the Boy Scouts of America,

443
00:45:22,000 --> 00:45:24,000
nor are they endorsed or sponsored by the Boy Scouts of America.

444
00:45:25,000 --> 00:45:29,000
Hey, it's just me talking into a microphone, trying to lend a hand to scout leaders out there,

445
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:32,000
and maybe have a little bit of fun along the way.

446
00:45:32,000 --> 00:45:45,000
Well, before we leave, let me remind you that, yes, that was a rebroadcast.

447
00:45:46,000 --> 00:45:48,000
We are rebroadcasting it because it's the middle of the summer,

448
00:45:49,000 --> 00:45:50,000
and we're off traveling and scouting.

449
00:45:51,000 --> 00:45:53,000
Gonna have plenty of stories to tell you once we get back home,

450
00:45:54,000 --> 00:45:55,000
and that'll be in another week or two.

451
00:45:56,000 --> 00:45:57,000
So thanks for continuing to listen,

452
00:45:58,000 --> 00:46:01,000
and before we go, let's hear from our founder, Sir Robert Baden Powell.

453
00:46:02,000 --> 00:46:04,000
Good luck to you and good camping.

454
00:46:05,000 --> 00:46:07,000
Well, thank you, sir. Until next time.

455
00:46:32,000 --> 00:46:34,000
Thank you.

456
00:47:02,000 --> 00:47:05,000
you

